      "headline": "The swallowable camera that made the colonoscopy optional",
      "lede": "A 968-patient head-to-head trial found the PillCam Colon 2 capsule caught 88% of significant polyps with no sedation and no proceduralist — now FDA-approved for people who skip colonoscopy.",
      "whyItMatters": "Colorectal cancer is the second-leading cause of cancer death, but fewer than 40% of people get screened — because the test itself deters them. A no-sedation, no-prep capsule targets exactly the people who opt out.",
      "bodyHtml": "<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\">PillCam Colon 2 is a capsule roughly the size of a large multivitamin, fitted with a camera at each end that photographs the entire colon as it travels through the gastrointestinal tract. There is no sedation, no proceduralist, and no recovery room — the images transmit wirelessly to a recorder worn at the waist and are reviewed later by a gastroenterologist.</p><p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\">In the Phase 3 trial, 968 patients underwent both the capsule and conventional colonoscopy on the same day, with endoscopists blinded to the capsule's findings. For polyps 6mm or larger — the threshold above which removal is recommended — the capsule reached 88% sensitivity. For advanced adenomas, the lesions most likely to progress to cancer, sensitivity was 87%. Specificity was 82%, and no serious adverse events were attributed to the capsule.</p><p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\">Those numbers do not match colonoscopy's near-perfect detection, and that is not the point. The clinical question is not whether the capsule beats colonoscopy; it is whether a capsule in a patient who would otherwise skip screening entirely beats no test at all. On that question, the answer is categorical.</p><p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\">The device has limits. It cannot biopsy tissue or remove polyps, so any positive finding still requires a follow-up colonoscopy. A lighter bowel-prep protocol is still needed, and roughly 5% of capsules fail to reach the cecum before the battery expires, requiring a repeat. An adaptive frame-rate algorithm — slowing capture where transit is fast — is designed to reduce missed lesions at bends and in the cecum.</p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-stretch: normal; line-height: normal;\">\n\n\n\n</p><p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\">The FDA has approved PillCam Colon 2 for patients unable or unwilling to undergo conventional colonoscopy, and the American College of Gastroenterology now lists it as an acceptable alternative for average-risk patients. Medtronic is running a post-market registry to measure real-world detection across varying prep compliance. The decisive question is behavioral, not clinical: if a no-sedation capsule lifts screening rates in populations that currently opt out, the drop in late-stage diagnoses would be substantial — and that evidence will come from registries, not trials.</p><p></p>",
